  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Building the View**\n\nI\'m starting to build out the EstimatorView component, focusing on the core display elements. Currently, I\'m thinking about including details like the current estimate, specifically, `EST-2409-A` for James Robertson at `1244 Maplewood Dr, Austin, TX 78704`, and an interactive items section.\n\n\n**Designing the Table**\n\nI\'m now focused on the interactive table for estimate items. I plan to enable editing quantities and unit prices, with dynamic recalculation of subtotals and the final total. I also intend to allow adding, deleting, and enabling/disabling a warranty addendum with custom terms. Finally, I\'m working on a margin indicator with an \"Auto-ajustar al 30%\" button to adjust labor pricing for a target 30% margin.\n\n\n**Calculating the Adjustment**\n\nI\'m deep in the margin calculation now, aiming for an elegant \"Auto-adjust to 30%\" feature. My current formula hinges on dynamically computing a target labor unit price to hit that 30% margin precisely. I\'m double-checking the figures and how expenses contribute to the overall costs of a project; it seems this approach is cleaner than the previous. I\'m now testing the calculation and how the new Labor Price value alters the subtotal, margin and final values.\n\n\n**Refining the Formula**\n\nI\'m now refining the profit margin calculation for accuracy and clarity. I\'ve landed on a formula: `(Total Estimate Gross - Cost of Materials) / Total Estimate Gross * 100`. I tested with the numbers, like when the subtotal gross is $12,045 and materials cost $7,420, resulting in a 38.3% margin. I\'m focusing on defining a cost basis for products, like shingles at $80/SQ sold at $120. My current focus is to better reflect a more realistic profit margin.\n\n\n**Analyzing the Figures**\n\nI\'ve been analyzing the profit margin formula and calculations in detail, focusing on tweaking costs to achieve a specific margin, like the target 24.5%. My calculations show that to hit this, I\'ll need to fine-tune crew costs and material costs to balance the equation. I\'m building out scenarios with shingles, underlayment, venting, and labor, calculating revenue, cost, and ultimately, the profit margin, aiming for precise adjustments. I\'m exploring different crew cost scenarios to determine the impact on the labor sell price and the resulting margin. I\'m working to make the numbers sync up.\n\n\n"
    modified_response: "I will now create `/src/components/EstimatorView.tsx` to build the interactive Claims Estimator interface. This component displays the active line-item table where users can edit values and see live-calculated financials, toggle warranties, and trigger the AI-recommended profit margin auto-adjuster (which raises labor prices to meet the target company standard)."
    thinking_duration {
      seconds: 16
      nanos: 892970666
    }
    stop_reason: STOP_REASON_STOP_PATTERN
  }
}
